From a39ed04d89a2434eb50efd09d52bfb16b515b14e Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Nicole Muzzin <nicole@muzz.in>
Date: Sun, 4 Oct 2026 05:58:13 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] media: hold back the frames of videos that don't paint

Each decoded frame of a video is copied into new shared memory, copied
again on the embedder's main thread and uploaded. Five muted, looping
2920x2000 videos with one visible (slack.com's hero) sent ~23 MB per
frame per video, the main thread fell behind and the window stopped
repainting and taking input.

The document now keeps its connected video elements and, after each
rendering update, tells each frame renderer whether the element paints:
it has a box, visibility: visible, and neither it nor an ancestor has
opacity: 0. A renderer that doesn't paint keeps the newest frame (still
available to drawImage()) instead of sending it, and sends it as soon as
the element paints again, which then repaints. Such a video also stops
marking itself dirty for every frame. Elements are assumed to paint until
the first rendering update says otherwise.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
